# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""English translations of the folk medicine stories (halk.hikayeler())."""

H_EN = {
 'agri-nin-diyadin-dogubayazit-yorelerinde-sifali': dict(
  baslik='When are herbs gathered in Ağrı?',
  yore='',
  post='In the Diyadin and Doğubayazıt areas of Ağrı, medicinal herbs start to be gathered in April and May, when they sprout. According to informants, most herbs lose their strength in August when they go to seed, so gathering ends in late July. Herbs are not gathered on rainy, very sunny or windy days.',
  kisa='Doğan, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 'dogubayazit-diyadin-otlari-toplayip-hastalara-verenler': dict(
  baslik='Mountain healers: women and shepherds',
  yore='',
  post='In Doğubayazıt and Diyadin, the people who gather herbs and give them to the sick are mostly women; women living in the highland pastures and shepherds also carry this knowledge. One shepherd interviewed says he learned which herb is good for which ailment, and when to gather it, by going up the mountain many times with his elders. The knowledge is passed on not through books but by walking together.',
  kisa='Doğan, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 'agri-nin-diyadin-dogubayazit-yorelerinde-civanpercemi': dict(
  baslik='Flour sprinkled on wounds: yarrow',
  yore='Ağrı (Diyadin, Doğubayazıt)',
  post='In the Diyadin and Doğubayazıt areas of Ağrı, yarrow is dried, ground like flour and sprinkled on open wounds. This plant takes its name from Achilles, and its use on wounds appears among many peoples, from Anatolia to North America.',
  kisa='Doğan, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 'diyadin-dogubayazit-hatmi-yalnizca-oksurukte-icilmiyor': dict(
  baslik='Marshmallow is not only for coughs',
  yore='Ağrı (Diyadin, Doğubayazıt)',
  post='In Diyadin and Doğubayazıt, marshmallow is not only drunk for coughs: for back pain, the dried herb is steeped and massaged onto the painful spot, often together with cupping. A folk treatment that uses the plant, the cup and the hand together.',
  kisa='Doğan, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 'agri-yoresinde-isirganin-kaynatma-suyu-yalnizca': dict(
  baslik='Nettle water for falling hair',
  yore='Ağrı (Diyadin, Doğubayazıt)',
  post='In the Ağrı region, the water in which stinging nettle is boiled is not only drunk; it is also applied to the hair regularly against hair loss. Nettle is also a plant often mentioned for hair care in European folk tradition.',
  kisa='Doğan, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 'her-papatya-ayni-bitki-degil': dict(
  baslik='Not every chamomile is chamomile',
  yore='Ağrı (Diyadin, Doğubayazıt)',
  post='Not every “chamomile” is the same plant. The plant called chamomile in the Doğubayazıt area is Anthemis armeniaca; only the flower heads are dried and boiled, and the steam is inhaled for sinusitis. The familiar tea chamomile is Matricaria chamomilla. Local names show why using a plant without knowing it is risky.',
  kisa='Doğan, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 'diyadin-dogubayazit-ebegumecinin-suyunu-cocuk-isteyen': dict(
  baslik='The herb of women who want a child',
  yore='Ağrı (Diyadin, Doğubayazıt)',
  post='In Diyadin and Doğubayazıt, women who want a child regularly drink the juice of common mallow. In Anatolia, mallow is mostly known as a food and as a cough remedy; this record shows that the same plant takes on quite different meanings from region to region.',
  kisa='Doğan, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 'uzerlik-anadolu-cogunlukla-nazarlikla-anilir-kapiya': dict(
  baslik='An amulet at the door, poison in the mouth',
  yore='Ağrı (Diyadin, Doğubayazıt)',
  post='In Anatolia, Syrian rue is mostly associated with charms against the evil eye; it is hung on doors and its seeds are burned as incense. In the Ağrı region, however, its seeds are also taken by mouth. Caution: Syrian rue seeds contain strong alkaloids that can cause poisoning; this is a cultural record, not a recommendation.',
  kisa='Doğan, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 'diyadin-dogubayazit-dag-sakizi-scorzonera-latifolia': dict(
  baslik='Milk from the root: mountain gum',
  yore='',
  post='In Diyadin and Doğubayazıt, “dağ sakızı” (mountain gum, Scorzonera latifolia) is made like this: the root is cut, the milky sap that flows out is collected in a jar, boiled while being stirred and left to harden. The resulting gum is chewed for stomach ache. What is made from the plant is not a “medicine” but a craft product.',
  kisa='Doğan, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 'agri-yoresinde-sigirdili-anchusa-azurea-yaylaya': dict(
  baslik='The herb burned after the summer pasture',
  yore='',
  post='In the Ağrı region, when people come back from the highland pastures, common bugloss (Anchusa azurea) is burned in the houses that stood empty; the smoke is meant to kill insects. The same record also warns that the plant can poison if used too much. An everyday, practical form of cleansing a house with plant smoke.',
  kisa='Doğan, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 'agri-yoresinde-dadirak-salvia-verticillata-mide': dict(
  baslik='The herb that fattens lambs: dadırak',
  yore='',
  post='In the Ağrı region, dadırak (Salvia verticillata) is used for stomach ache and is also fed to thin lambs; the lambs are said to gain weight. A warning also passes from mouth to mouth in the region: “If consumed too much, it causes death.” Folk knowledge carries the importance of the dose in its own words.',
  kisa='Doğan, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 'dogubayazit-yoresinde-agriyan-iltihapli-dis-sinir': dict(
  baslik='One night for toothache: plantain',
  yore='',
  post='In the Doğubayazıt area, for an aching, inflamed tooth, greater plantain (Plantago major) is crushed, placed on the tooth and left until morning; its leaf is also stuck onto inflamed wounds. Plantain leaf on wounds appears among many peoples, from Europe to the Americas.',
  kisa='Doğan, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 'manisa-turgutlu-nun-akcapinar-koyunde-otu': dict(
  baslik='A healing hearth still at work: Otu Kesme',
  yore='',
  post='In Akçapınar village in Turgutlu, Manisa, the “Otu Kesme Ocağı” (Otu Kesme healing hearth) still works today. In 2020 researchers observed the hearth and interviewed the healer and patients: people go there mostly for skin diseases, and patients come from nearby villages and districts. According to the healer, the condition for recovery is following the method given.',
  kisa='Özçiftçi, Üstün, Ersoy, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 'anadolu-ocak-sifa-gucunun-ailede-kusaktan': dict(
  baslik='Does healing run in the blood? Ocaks',
  yore='',
  post='In Anatolia, an “ocak” (hearth) is a tradition in which healing power is believed to pass from generation to generation within a family. Medical historians who studied the Otu Kesme Ocağı in Akçapınar also consulted specialist physicians in the relevant fields about the soil, plants and diseases involved. Taking folk medicine seriously means both recording it and questioning it.',
  kisa='Özçiftçi, Üstün, Ersoy, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 '1984-eczacilik-tarihcileri-anadolu-bas-agrisina': dict(
  baslik='28 folk remedies for headache',
  yore='',
  post='In 1984, pharmacy historians compiled from oral accounts the folk remedies used against headache in Central Anatolia: of 28 substances, 24 are plant-based, 3 animal-based and 1 mineral. Of these 28, only 4 are still used in modern medicine; active compounds have been obtained from 5.',
  kisa='Asil, Tanker, Şar, Ankara Ü. Eczacılık Fak. Dergisi, 1984'),
 'dilek-dileyip-dut-agacinin-dallarina-bez': dict(
  baslik='Wishes tied to mulberry branches',
  yore='',
  post='Making a wish and tying a cloth to the branches of a mulberry tree is, according to Ülkü Gürsoy, a custom rooted in the old Turkic tree cult. In the Alevi-Bektashi tradition the mulberry tree kept its sacredness and was regarded as “the soul of the house.” Many things we call “custom” today have roots that reach back to the steppe.',
  kisa='Gürsoy, Türk Kültürü ve Hacı Bektaş Veli Araştırma Dergisi, 2013'),
 'mersin-mut-uzerlik-tohumu-tuzla-kozde': dict(
  baslik='Syrian rue on embers, smoke in the rooms',
  yore='Mersin (Mut), Adana (Karaisalı), Sivas, Gaziantep (Nizip)',
  post='In Mut, Mersin, Syrian rue seeds are burned with salt on embers, and the smoke is carried through every room of the house against the evil eye. A local saying: “A snake visits a hundred houses in one night, but not the house with Syrian rue.” In Karaisalı people jump over the smoke, and the ash is buried far from the house. Note: Syrian rue seed is poisonous; it is especially dangerous for pregnant women.',
  kisa='Kırıcı et al., Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2018'),
 'anadolu-uzerlik-yalnizca-nazardan-korumak-degil': dict(
  baslik='The seed that measures the evil eye',
  yore='',
  post='In Anatolia, Syrian rue is used not only to protect against the evil eye but also to “measure” it: at healing hearths the seeds are thrown onto a hot griddle, and the more they crack, the stronger the evil eye is believed to be. In Kayseri the seeds are burned with brushwood gathered at a crossroads; in Van, with measured threads and the name of the person suspected of casting the evil eye.',
  kisa='Güçlü, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2026'),
 'cocuklarin-boynuna-asilan-nazarlik-keselerine-uzerlik': dict(
  baslik='What is in the evil-eye pouch?',
  yore='',
  post='The evil-eye pouches hung around children’s necks hold Syrian rue seeds, and also black pepper, garlic skin and black cumin. The reason is the belief that sharp smells “break” the evil eye. In Alanya, prayer beads and necklaces are made from the seeds of Christ’s thorn, and its branches are hung on doors, cars and wedding horses.',
  kisa='Güçlü, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2026'),
 'mus-anlatilan-efsane-lokman-hekim-carsida': dict(
  baslik='Why did Lokman Hekim leave this city?',
  yore='Muş (Kurtik Dağı)',
  post='A legend told in Muş: a lokman hekim (healer) saw gulik (çiriş otu, foxtail lily) on sale everywhere in the market, thought “These people will fall ill often” and left the city. The saying still lives in the region: “The people of Muş fall ill from gulik and get well from ışkın.” Işkın is ışgın (Rheum ribes), gathered in the mountains in spring.',
  kisa='Kardaş, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2019'),
 'mus-yilanyastigi-arum-elongatum-yorede-kari': dict(
  baslik='The herb in the bride’s trousseau',
  yore='',
  post='In Muş, yılanyastığı (Arum elongatum, locally karî-kardî) was once put into brides’ trousseaus and taken along on visits to a new bride; its presence was a sign of preparing for a child. According to some people of the Elmani tribe, however, whoever gathers it brings serious illness to someone in their family. Note: yılanyastığı is a poisonous plant.',
  kisa='Kardaş, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2019'),
 'mus-varto-ilcesinde-caksir-ferula-rigidula': dict(
  baslik='The herb of the twins’ village',
  yore='',
  post='In the Varto district of Muş, çakşır (Ferula rigidula, locally cağ) has one more name: “ikiziksiri” (twin elixir). Local people attribute the birth of about a hundred twins in Kaynarca village to this plant; it is said that çakşır both brings twins and gives a child to those who have none.',
  kisa='Kardaş, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2019'),
 'mus-hatmi-cicegi-alcea-yorede-hiro': dict(
  baslik='Hollyhock in the years without soap',
  yore='Muş',
  post='In Muş, hollyhock (Alcea, locally hîro) lathers, so in years of poverty it was used instead of soap to wash the dead. For snakebite, its stem, flower and leaf are pounded, mixed with ayran (a yogurt drink) and applied for seven days. The slippery mucilage in hollyhock roots is also the source of its lather.',
  kisa='Kardaş, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2019'),
 'anadolu-dogum-odasinda-kase-icinde-kuru': dict(
  baslik='The flower that opens in the birth room',
  yore='',
  post='In Anatolia, a bowl of water in the birth room with a dry plant in it: Fatma Ana Eli (“Mother Fatma’s Hand”, rose of Jericho, Anastatica hierochuntica). As the plant opens in the water, the birth is believed to become easy, like water. In some regions the midwife rubs the woman’s back and says: “This hand is not my hand, it is the hand of our Mother Fatma.” Note: drinking this plant’s water in pregnancy has not been proven safe.',
  kisa='Şen and Dinç Kaya, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2021'),
 'ayni-bitki-farkli-adlar-anastatica-hierochuntica': dict(
  baslik='Fadime Ana, Meryem Ana, Havva Ana',
  yore='',
  post='One plant, different names: in Ankara, Sivas and Tokat, Anastatica hierochuntica (rose of Jericho) is called Fadime Ana, Meryem Ana or Havva Ana (Mother Fatima, Mother Mary, Mother Eve). As it opens in water, the “doors of wishes” are believed to open too. In a study in Karaman, 77.9 percent of women said they had used this plant to ease childbirth.',
  kisa='Şen and Dinç Kaya, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2021'),
 'hititler-ardici-dini-torenlerde-kullanmis-mezar': dict(
  baslik='Sacred tree of the Hittites: juniper',
  yore='Hittites',
  post='The Hittites used juniper in religious ceremonies and also in building tombs. For loss of appetite, the patient was given şeytan teresi (giant fennel, Ferula communis) for seven days; if there was no improvement, other plants such as garlic were tried. Which plant would go into a remedy was sometimes decided by divination.',
  kisa='Yeğenoğlu et al., Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2016'),
 'hititlerin-yaklasik-bayramindan-biri-antahsum-adini': dict(
  baslik='A festival named after a crocus',
  yore='Hittites',
  post='ANTAHŞUM, one of the roughly 18 Hittite festivals, took its name from a crocus and celebrated the renewal of plant life. That a plant’s name became the name of a festival shows how important the plant was to that society. Note: autumn crocus is a very poisonous plant.',
  kisa='Yeğenoğlu et al., Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2016'),
 'adamotu-soylencesi-koku-topraktan-sokulurken-ciglik': dict(
  baslik='The root that screams when pulled',
  yore='Antiquity',
  post='The mandrake legend: when its root is pulled from the ground it screams, and whoever hears the sound goes deaf or dies. So the root was tied to a dog, and the sound was drowned out with drums; the scene is pictured in a Dioscorides manuscript. In the Torah, Rachel and Leah trade mandrakes, and Leah has a child. Note: mandrake is very poisonous.',
  kisa='Tarhan et al., Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2016'),
 'naneye-adini-veren-peri-yunan-soylencesinde': dict(
  baslik='The nymph who became mint',
  yore='Ancient Greece',
  post='Mint is named after a nymph: in Greek myth, the water nymph Menthe, lover of Hades, god of the underworld, is turned into the mint plant. The name of the peony (Paeonia) also comes from Paeon, a physician who was turned into a plant. In Egypt, peony root was moved in the shape of a cross over people having epileptic fits, and it was called “haçotu” (cross herb).',
  kisa='Tarhan et al., Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2016'),
 'mugla-ula-kok-boyaci-recep-tutuncu': dict(
  baslik='The dyer’s burn ointment',
  yore='Muğla (Ula)',
  post='In Ula, Muğla, the madder dyer Recep Tütüncü (1865–1950) made his own mixture when burns from the dye cauldrons became frequent: kiriş otu root, the fine roots of valonia oak, chaste tree bark and mastic tree resin, with vegetable oils. The knowledge passed to his son, then to his grandchildren. If a large part of the body is burned, they send the patient to hospital.',
  kisa='Birgili and Özcan, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2012'),
 'turk-halk-hekimliginde-yaklasik-150-ocak': dict(
  baslik='150 kinds of healing hearth',
  yore='',
  post='About 150 kinds of ocak (healing hearth) have been recorded in Turkish folk medicine. At a hernia hearth in Balıkesir, a rose branch is split lengthwise without being cut from the plant, the patient is passed through the gap while prayers are recited, and the branch is bound up again with cotton thread. As the branch grows back together, the hernia is believed to heal too.',
  kisa='Acar, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2017'),
 'denizli-deki-devre-ocaginda-yara-hastanin': dict(
  baslik='As the potato dries, the wound dries',
  yore='',
  post='At the devre hearth in Denizli, for a wound, a potato brought by the patient is cut into three slices, strung on a thread and hung in the house; as the potato dries, the wound is believed to dry too. At the çıbık hearth in Konya, a grapevine shoot is made into a ring, and a child with red eyes and nose is passed through the ring.',
  kisa='Acar, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2017'),
 'ordu-giresun-taflan-karayemis-laurocerasus-officinalis': dict(
  baslik='Leaves on the barn ceiling',
  yore='',
  post='In Ordu and Giresun, taflan (karayemiş, cherry laurel, Laurocerasus officinalis) is winter fodder for animals: the leaves are hung from the barn ceiling. In Keşap, for teat warts in a cow, the leaves are rubbed on the udder for seven days; meanwhile people walk around the animal reciting Surah al-Ikhlas, and then a leaf is left beside the animal for three days.',
  kisa='Karasu and Yaşar, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2023'),
 'laden-cistus-kaya-gulu-pamukluk-tavsancil': dict(
  baslik='Resin combed from goat hair',
  yore='',
  post='The resin of laden (rockrose, Cistus; also kaya gülü, pamukluk, tavşancıl) used to stick to the fleece of goats and sheep grazing among the plants, and was collected by combing the fleece. Today leafy branches are boiled in water and the resin that sinks to the bottom is collected. Laden is one of the three plants most collected from the wild in Turkey.',
  kisa='Şekeroğlu and Gezici, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2021'),
 '600-yillik-sac-bakimi-haci-pasa': dict(
  baslik='600 years of hair care',
  yore='14th–15th-century Turkish medical texts',
  post='600 years of hair care: 14th- and 15th-century physicians such as Hacı Paşa and Sabuncuoğlu recommend myrtle oil for hair loss. In one recipe, myrtle leaves, oak galls and basil are boiled, and sesame oil and laden (rockrose resin) are added; the mixture is rubbed into the hair roots every night and washed out in the morning with myrtle water.',
  kisa='Serarslan and Altıntaş, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2026'),
 'yuzyilda-yazilan-ilk-turkce-ilac-kitaplarindan': dict(
  baslik='Saffron in the first Turkish drug book',
  yore='14th–16th-century Turkish medical texts',
  post='In Edviye-i Müfrede, one of the first Turkish drug books, written in the 14th century, saffron is counted as “hot and dry” in temperament and is said to strengthen the stomach, but with a warning: too much causes headache. Saffron also went into the strength pastes of Ottoman sultans. And Safranbolu takes its name from this plant.',
  kisa='Paşayeva and Tekiner, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2014'),
 'edirne-sarayi-nin-gulhane-sinde-hem': dict(
  baslik='The palace’s rose water',
  yore='',
  post='The Gülhane of Edirne Palace had both a rose garden and a building where rose water was made; at the Gülhane in Topkapı, too, rose water was made from roses grown in the palace. The 15th-century medical book Kemaliye recommends rubbing rose powder on the damp neck after the hammam. In the Ottoman period, rose oil also had a place in training to memorize the Quran (hafızlık).',
  kisa='Güler and Karataş, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2014'),
 'mevlana-uzum-yolculuktur-koruk-olgun-uzum': dict(
  baslik='The grape’s journey in Mevlânâ',
  yore='',
  post='In Mevlânâ, the grape is a journey: unripe grape, ripe grape and wine stand for the stages of knowing, finding and becoming. A dervish who has reached the secret of existence is called “a dervish who has become a grape.” The roses in the garden of his tomb in Konya were planted by order of his son, to keep his father’s love of roses alive.',
  kisa='Yeğenoğlu and Sözen Şahne, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2016'),
 'meryemana-dikeninin-yapraklarindaki-beyaz-damarlar-soylenceye': dict(
  baslik='A drop of milk on the leaf',
  yore='Christian legend; Turkey',
  post='The white veins on the leaves of milk thistle are linked to a legend: while Mary was nursing Jesus under this plant, a drop of her milk fell on the leaves. In Turkey the plant has many names: deve kengeri, sütlü kengel, akdiken, mübarek diken, şevkülmeryem.',
  kisa='Ereni and Şar, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2020'),
 'tere-lepidium-sativum-anadolu-kerdeme-circir': dict(
  baslik='Kerdeme, cırcır, garden cress',
  yore='',
  post='Garden cress (Lepidium sativum) is also known in Anatolia as kerdeme and cırcır. In folk use its leaf goes into salads, its seed is used as a spice, and its crushed seed is rubbed on warts. The same source also gives a warning: taking too much of the seed can cause disorders in the body.',
  kisa='Başağaoğlu and Kavalalı, Lokman Hekim Dergisi, 2019'),
 '1928-2014-arasinda-turkiye-yapilmis-930': dict(
  baslik='930 studies, one database',
  yore='',
  post='930 ethnobotanical studies carried out in Turkey between 1928 and 2014 were brought together in a single database. The most often recorded plants: stinging nettle (178 publications), dog rose (157), walnut (136), St John’s wort (112), greater plantain (112) and common mallow (109). All are ordinary plants found along village roads and garden edges.',
  kisa='Öztürk et al., Türkiye’nin Etnobotanik Veritabanı, 2020'),
 'kusburnu-turkiye-kadar-yoresel-kayitli': dict(
  baslik='The 60 names of the dog rose',
  yore='',
  post='About 60 local names are recorded in Turkey for kuşburnu (dog rose): itburnu, köpek gülü, it gülü, öküzgötü, şilan, gülburnu, deligül, gelin göbeği, gülbubusu, dikenbaşı… The same plant, a different name in each region. What is it called where you live?',
  kisa='Öztürk et al., Türkiye’nin Etnobotanik Veritabanı, 2020'),
 'anadolu-yara-otu-adi-tek-bitkiye': dict(
  baslik='Which wound herb?',
  yore='',
  post='In Anatolia the name “yara otu” (wound herb) does not belong to a single plant: in Kastamonu it is given to St John’s wort, in Ankara to boz otu, in Balıkesir, Bolu and Giresun to greater plantain, and in Bilecik to Prunella species. A local name alone is not enough to identify a plant; that is why the Latin name matters.',
  kisa='Öztürk et al., Türkiye’nin Etnobotanik Veritabanı, 2020'),
 'turkiye-deki-halk-kayitlarinda-bitkilerin-cok': dict(
  baslik='What troubles people most?',
  yore='',
  post='In folk records in Turkey, the complaint plants are most often used for is hemorrhoids (367 different plants), followed by stomach ache. For the skin, wound healing is far ahead in first place: 467 plants, 1,093 records. These numbers do not show effectiveness; they show how common the complaint is.',
  kisa='Öztürk et al., Türkiye’nin Etnobotanik Veritabanı, 2020'),
 'dis-kurdu-inanci-kuzeydogu-anadolu-dis': dict(
  baslik='Worms falling from the tooth',
  yore='Northeast and East Anatolia',
  post='The tooth worm belief: in Northeast Anatolia, a person with toothache puts their head under a blanket, henbane seeds are sprinkled on embers, and the smoke is drawn in through the mouth. It is said that the small worms rotting the tooth fall into the bowl of water below. Researchers note that the tooth worm belief has its origins in Anatolia. Caution: henbane is poisonous, and its smoke is dangerous too.',
  kisa='Sezik et al., J Ethnopharmacol, 1991'),
 'iki-ayri-agac-tek-karadeniz-taflan': dict(
  baslik='One bay, one poison: two taflans',
  yore='Northwest Anatolia',
  post='Two different trees, one name: on the Black Sea, “taflan” usually means cherry laurel (Laurocerasus officinalis). People who moved from the eastern Black Sea also planted this tree in their new villages. But in some villages bay laurel is also called “taflan” and is used for other things. Researchers warn: cherry laurel leaves and seeds are poisonous, especially in large amounts; one must not be used in place of the other because of the shared name.',
  kisa='Yeşilada et al., J Ethnopharmacol, 1999'),
 'tugla-yagi-sakarya-nin-koyunde-romatizma': dict(
  baslik='Can a brick give oil?',
  yore='Sakarya',
  post='Brick oil: in a village in Sakarya, for rheumatic pain, two dry bricks are pounded into powder, roasted in a pan with pure olive oil, and water is added. The oil that rises to the surface is collected with a spoon and kept in a bottle. In the record, the product has that name too: brick oil.',
  kisa='Yeşilada et al., J Ethnopharmacol, 1999'),
 'temre-rituel-kuzeybati-anadolu-mantar-kaynakli': dict(
  baslik='A Saturday prayer for temre',
  yore='Northwest Anatolia',
  post='Rituals for temre: in Northwest Anatolia there are countless rituals for “temre”, a skin disease caused by fungi. In one village a prayer is written over the patch with a heather twig sharpened like a pen; in other villages the edge of the patch is traced with a hot needle, rock salt or a barley stalk. In some places this is done only on Saturdays.',
  kisa='Yeşilada et al., J Ethnopharmacol, 1999'),
 'kara-hekim-toroslar-cam-ardic-odunundan': dict(
  baslik='The black healer of the Taurus',
  yore='İç Toroslar (Inner Taurus)',
  post='“Kara hekim” (black healer): in the Inner Taurus, tar made in the traditional way from pine and juniper wood is called the black healer, for its dark color and the healing power it is believed to have. For sunstroke at harvest time, this is done: pine tar or garlic yogurt is rubbed on the body, covered with fresh leaves and wrapped in a blanket, and the person is made to sweat for two or three hours.',
  kisa='Yeşilada et al., J Ethnopharmacol, 1995'),
 'mekke-tasi-aydin-yilancik-erizipel-ocagi': dict(
  baslik='The Mecca stone that grows in flour',
  yore='Aydın',
  post='The Mecca stone: in Aydın, a holder of an erysipelas (yılancık) hearth diagnosed with a “stone” a grandfather had brought from Mecca a century ago. If it stuck to the wound, the disease was erysipelas. The healer said the stone was kept in a box with corn and wheat flour and grew there. Researchers think it was most likely a mollusk shell; as a hearth secret, it was not given for study.',
  kisa='Honda et al., J Ethnopharmacol, 1996'),
 'yasindaki-kirikci-mugla-arastirmacilarin-gorustugu-yasindaki': dict(
  baslik='The 95-year-old bonesetter',
  yore='Muğla',
  post='The 95-year-old bonesetter: in Muğla, a 95-year-old bonesetter interviewed by researchers held the set bone in place with a mixture of bitter vetch seed, egg yolk and grated soap. In western Anatolia, this kind of skill for a single complaint, such as binding fractures or treating erysipelas, is also called an “ocak”, and it is usually passed down from parents.',
  kisa='Honda et al., J Ethnopharmacol, 1996'),
 'agri-kadin-sifaci-adini-koyamadigi-hastaliklar': dict(
  baslik='A cauldron of herbs for a nameless illness',
  yore='Ağrı',
  post='In Ağrı, for internal illnesses she could not name, a woman healer boiled herbs known locally as pılınk (wild mint), belhavz (greater plantain), gezgezk (stinging nettle) and gelenkotu in a large cauldron, and the patient was washed with this water for a few days. She also gave a tea of the same herbs and common mallow to drink. She had learned this from her mother.',
  kisa='Sezik et al., Economic Botany, 1997'),
 'kastamonu-halk-ilaclarini-bilenlere-bilgiyi-nereden': dict(
  baslik='Who did we learn healing from?',
  yore='Kastamonu',
  post='In Kastamonu, people who knew folk remedies were asked where they had learned them: 78 percent from their parents and village elders, only 8.5 percent from health workers. One of the records is St John’s wort: the fresh flowers are pounded and put on open wounds, or a tea of the flowering herb is drunk for stomach ache, two glasses a day.',
  kisa='Sezik, Zor, Yeşilada, Int J Pharmacogn, 1992'),
 'orta-cag-salgin-donemlerinde-hekimler-yuzlerine': dict(
  baslik='The garlic mask',
  yore='From antiquity to Anatolia',
  post='In the Middle Ages, during epidemics, physicians are said to have soaked the masks they wore in garlic juice. Herodotus also writes that onions, radishes and garlic were handed out to the workers building the pyramid of Cheops. An old method in Anatolia for expelling worms: a peeled clove of garlic is rubbed well into a piece of bread crust, and the bread, having taken in the smell, is eaten.',
  kisa='Baytop, Türkiye’de Bitkiler ile Tedavi (Plant Healing in Turkey), 2021'),
 'kuyumcularin-kirat-keciboynuzundan-gelir': dict(
  baslik='The carat is a seed',
  yore='From antiquity to today',
  post='The jewelers’ “carat” comes from the carob. Its seeds were once used as a unit of weight by jewelers; the word comes from the names given to the seed, Arabic qirat and ancient Greek keration. Today one carat, used in weighing diamonds and emeralds, equals 0.2 grams.',
  kisa='Baytop, Türkiye’de Bitkiler ile Tedavi (Plant Healing in Turkey), 2021'),
 'avrupa-turk-kirmizisi-edirne-kirmizisi-diye': dict(
  baslik='The root that dyed the world',
  yore='Ottoman period',
  post='The dye known in Europe as “Turkey red” or “Edirne red” was obtained from the root of madder. In the 1700s Anatolia alone supplied two-thirds of the world’s madder; the best came from the Bakır çayı plain in Kırkağaç and was known as “Bakır.” After the dye began to be made synthetically in 1869, this trade soon died out.',
  kisa='Baytop, Türkiye’de Bitkiler ile Tedavi (Plant Healing in Turkey), 2021'),
 'yuzyilda-bursali-ali-munsi-avrupa-kaynaklarindan': dict(
  baslik='How cinchona came into Turkish',
  yore='18th-century Ottoman Empire',
  post='In the 18th century, Bursalı Ali Münşi drew on European sources to write a Turkish monograph on cinchona bark. This bark, used against malaria, had reached Europe after Europeans entered Peru after 1513. After 1884, the main production shifted to Dutch plantations in the East Indies.',
  kisa='Baytop, Türkiye’de Bitkiler ile Tedavi (Plant Healing in Turkey), 2021'),
 'osmanli-istanbul-unda-bazi-sifalar-sokakta': dict(
  baslik='Remedies sold on the street',
  yore='Ottoman İstanbul',
  post='In Ottoman İstanbul, some remedies were sold on the street. From street vendors, people bought thyme water for coughs and phlegm, mint water for nausea, and bitter orange blossom water as a calming drink that brings sleep. All were made by steam distillation of flowering branches; bitter orange blossom water was made from fresh flowers.',
  kisa='Baytop, Türkiye’de Bitkiler ile Tedavi (Plant Healing in Turkey), 2021'),
 'manisa-nin-yunt-dagi-koylerinde-arastirmacilar': dict(
  baslik='Three remedies of Yunt Dağı',
  yore='Manisa (Yunt Dağı)',
  post='In the villages of Yunt Dağı in Manisa, researchers interviewed 55 people; most people in the area are of Yörük origin. The plant mentioned most was mint: two-thirds of those interviewed named mint tea for stomach ache. Second was St John’s wort, rubbed on wounds with olive oil. In one village, children who wet the bed at night are given oriental sweetgum resin to chew.',
  kisa='Uğurlu and Seçmen, Fitoterapia, 2008'),
 'anadolu-nazara-karsi-tutsulenen-uzerligin-tohumlarinda': dict(
  baslik='What evil-eye rue shares with ayahuasca',
  yore='Anatolia and the Amazon',
  post='The seeds of Syrian rue, burned against the evil eye in Anatolia, contain harmine and harmaline. The same alkaloid family gives its effect to the vine that is the main ingredient of the ayahuasca drink in the Amazon. The ethnobotanist Richard Evans Schultes pointed out this link in 1969, but added that records of Syrian rue being used to have visions are doubtful.',
  kisa='Schultes, Science, 1969'),
 'peru-amazonu-nda-sifaci-olmak-isteyen': dict(
  baslik='Plants that teach',
  yore='Peruvian Amazon (Shipibo-Konibo)',
  post='In the Peruvian Amazon, a person who wants to become a healer enters a “dieta”: alone in the forest, they take one or a few plants each day and avoid salt, sugar, fat and alcohol. These are called “plants that have a mother”, because they are believed to teach. Shipibo-Konibo healers say one of the strongest is mapacho tobacco: its mother appears in dreams as a large, dark man in silver clothes.',
  kisa='Jauregui et al., J Ethnopharmacol, 2011'),
 'hakkari-nin-guneyindeki-sanidar-magarasinda-yaklasik': dict(
  baslik='A 60,000-year-old flower burial',
  yore='Shanidar Cave (Iraq), Middle Paleolithic',
  post='In Shanidar Cave, south of Hakkari, pollen of medicinal plants, ephedra among them, was found around a Neanderthal man buried about 60,000 years ago. The grave was called the “flower burial”, and it was even claimed to be a shaman’s. But some researchers think a rodent, the Persian jird, carried the pollen into the cave. So it is not certain that the plants were left there in a ritual.',
  kisa='Guerra-Doce, Time and Mind, 2015'),
 'ayahuasca-iki-bitkinin-birlesimidir-sarmasigin-kabugu': dict(
  baslik='Two plants, one drink',
  yore='Peruvian Amazon',
  post='Ayahuasca combines two plants: the bark of the vine and the leaf of chacruna. Taken by mouth, the DMT in the leaf does nothing on its own, because the MAO enzyme in digestion breaks it down. The harmine and harmaline in the vine block this enzyme for a time. In 1984 McKenna and colleagues studied this mechanism in eight samples from Peru. This is why ayahuasca can interact dangerously with some medicines.',
  kisa='McKenna et al., J Ethnopharmacol, 1984'),
 'zerdustlerin-kutsal-toreni-yasna-rahipler-haoma': dict(
  baslik='Which plant was haoma?',
  yore='Iran, Zoroastrian tradition',
  post='In Yasna, the most sacred Zoroastrian rite, priests prepare and drink “haoma”, a symbol of immortality, purification and the bond with God. Which plant it was made from is still debated. The strongest candidate is ephedra; the name is related to “soma” in the Indian Vedas. Some researchers propose Syrian rue, burned against the evil eye in Anatolia: its seeds contain harmine and harmaline. The answer is still unclear.',
  kisa='Zarei Karyani et al., Ethnobot Res Appl, 2025'),
 'yuzyilda-sirvan-dan-anadolu-gelen-hekim': dict(
  baslik='How was a fake drug detected?',
  yore='15th-century Ottoman Empire',
  post='Şirvânî, a 15th-century physician who came to Anatolia from Şirvan, explains in his pharmacy book how to tell a fake drug. Genuine turmeric peels in layers and tastes bitter, and inside its skin a dark core like blood can be seen; the fake is the opposite. Opium was tested by mixing it with water: if sediment stayed at the bottom it was fake, if not, it was genuine.',
  kisa='Şirvânî, Şifa Bahçesi (OGM publ.)'),
 'bolivya-magarada-bin-yillik-deri-kese': dict(
  baslik='A shaman’s thousand-year-old pouch',
  yore='Bolivian Andes, about 1,000 years ago',
  post='A thousand-year-old leather pouch was found in a cave in Bolivia. Inside were snuff trays, a snuff tube and a bag sewn from three fox snouts. The bag held traces of cocaine, DMT, bufotenine and harmine: coca, yopo and the ayahuasca vine. None of these plants grow there. The shaman who carried the pouch had them brought from far away, and may have used them together.',
  kisa='Miller et al., PNAS, 2019'),
 'pamir-deniz-seviyesinden-000-metre-yukseklikteki': dict(
  baslik='2,500-year-old cannabis braziers',
  yore='Pamir Plateau, about 2,500 years ago',
  post='In the Pamirs, at the Jirzankal cemetery 3,000 meters above sea level, wooden braziers and burnt stones were found. The braziers held traces of cannabis. In rites for the dead, cannabis may have been thrown on hot stones and the smoke inhaled. Herodotus also writes that the Scythians held a similar steam rite after funerals. This is the oldest known chemical evidence of cannabis smoke.',
  kisa='Ren et al., Science Advances, 2019'),
 'baytop-gore-acicigdem-kolhis-bugunku-batum': dict(
  baslik='Medea’s garden in Tarabya',
  yore='İstanbul, Tarabya',
  post='According to Baytop, autumn crocus grows abundantly in Colchis, around today’s Batumi. The name colchicine comes from there. The legendary sorceress Medea was also from Colchis. It is told that after fleeing her land she stayed in İstanbul for a time, set up a garden of medicinal plants on the Bosphorus and treated the people. The name of the district, Tarabya, is said to come from the Greek Therapia, “treatment.”',
  kisa='Baytop, Türkiye’de Bitkiler ile Tedavi (Plant Healing in Turkey), 2021'),
 'romalilar-doneminde-iyi-safran-silifke-deki': dict(
  baslik='Saffron of the Cehennem cave',
  yore='Silifke, Cehennem cave',
  post='In Roman times the best saffron grew in the thickets at the bottom of the Cehennem cave in Silifke. In 1995 Baytop went to the area to see if this still went on. No one was gathering saffron, and the Yörüks did not know zerde, the famous saffron dessert. Yet even the Hittites knew saffron; they called it azupiru, Akkadian for “saffron plant.”',
  kisa='Baytop, Türkiye’de Bitkiler ile Tedavi (Plant Healing in Turkey), 2021'),
 'adamotunun-meyvesi-hos-kokar-sari-yuvarlaktir': dict(
  baslik='The melon that dazes',
  yore='Silifke',
  post='The fruit of the mandrake smells pleasant and is yellow and round, so children sometimes eat it. But every part of the plant, especially the root, is poisonous; those who eat the fruit become dizzy and drunk. Baytop records that this is why in Silifke the plant is called “toskafakavunu” (the melon that dazes). In the same area it is also called “atelması” and “ittaşağı.”',
  kisa='Baytop, Türkiye’de Bitkiler ile Tedavi (Plant Healing in Turkey), 2021'),
 'orta-cag-avrupasi-nda-bira-cok': dict(baslik='Henbane in the beer', yore='Medieval Europe', kisa='Müller, Clin Toxicol, 1998',
   post='In medieval Europe henbane seed was added to beer to make it more intoxicating. In German henbane is Bilsenkraut, and the name of the Bohemian town of Pilsen is thought to come from it. The practice became so common that in 1507 Eichstätt banned brewers from adding henbane, and in 1649 Bavaria made it a severely punished offence. Henbane seed is poisonous.'),
 'orta-cag-cerrahlari-ameliyattan-once-uyku': dict(baslik='The medieval sleep sponge', yore='Medieval Europe', kisa='Müller, Clin Toxicol, 1998',
   post='Before an operation, medieval surgeons used a “sleep sponge”: a sponge was soaked in the juice of opium poppy, mandrake and henbane and dried, then moistened with hot water and laid over the patient’s mouth and nose. In 1363 the surgeon Guy de Chauliac warned that the method could cause lasting harm and death. To wake the patient there was a “wake-up sponge” soaked in fennel juice or vinegar.'),
 'sibirya-nin-bazi-halklarinda-sinek-mantari': dict(baslik='Fly agaric in Siberia', yore='Siberia', kisa='Michelot & Melendez-Howell, Mycol Res, 2003',
   post='Among some Siberian peoples a custom was recorded of drinking the urine of a shaman, or of a reindeer, that had eaten fly agaric. There is a reason: the mushroom’s active compounds leave the body partly unchanged in urine and are found there an hour after eating. The name is curious too: in many languages it is the “fly” mushroom; in the Middle Ages flies were believed to enter the head and cause madness. The mushroom is poisonous.'),
 'ronesans-bitki-kitaplarindaki-efsaneye-gore-adamotu': dict(baslik='The root that screams', yore='Medieval and Renaissance Europe', kisa='Müller, Clin Toxicol, 1998',
   post='According to a legend found in Renaissance herbals, mandrake grows beneath the gallows. When it is pulled up it gives such a scream that anyone who hears it dies. So the gatherer stopped his ears with wool, tied the plant by a rope to a dog’s neck and called the dog; the dog that pulled the plant out died. Shakespeare too writes in Romeo and Juliet of “shrieks like mandrakes torn out of the earth”.'),
 'hamlet-krali-uyurken-kulagina-hebenon-denen': dict(baslik='Hamlet’s poison', yore='Shakespeare’s Denmark', kisa='Müller, Clin Toxicol, 1998',
   post='In Hamlet the king is killed by a poison called “hebenon” poured into his ear while he sleeps. Today it is thought to have been henbane. Henbane was well known to poisoners and executioners in Europe: sixteenth-century accounts from Lucerne record the executioner buying henbane seed. All parts of henbane are poisonous.'),
 '1670-lerde-fransiz-sarayinda-aciklanamayan-olumler': dict(baslik='The affair of the poisons', yore='Seventeenth-century France', kisa='Müller, Clin Toxicol, 1998',
   post='When unexplained deaths multiplied at the French court in the 1670s, Louis XIV set up a special court to investigate poisonings. Between 1679 and 1683 more than 400 people were tried and 87 were sentenced to death as sorcerers and poisoners. The notorious Catherine Voisin, who sold love potions and poisons, laced drinks with henbane, thorn apple, Spanish fly and arsenic.'),
 'peru-amazonu-nda-derin-ormanda-chullachaqui': dict(baslik='The goat-footed lord of the forest', yore='Peruvian Amazon, Iquitos', kisa='Luna, J Ethnopharmacol, 1984',
   post='Deep in the forest of the Peruvian Amazon lives the Chullachaqui: he looks like a person, except that one foot is the foot of a goat, a deer or a dog. He is the lord of the animals, the “king of the forest”. Whoever befriends him hunts well; more often, those who meet him go mad or fall ill. Healers in Iquitos say he lives at the foot of the chullachaqui caspi tree, whose roots rise from the soil like a “foot”; this tree is also a teacher plant.'),
}
